## Principles

The visual starting point is the [Japanese *Sonic the Hedgehog* Mega Drive cover](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/): oversized compressed lettering, flat colour, an offset circle, diagonal fields and a small collection of geometric marks. The [V&A’s postmodern design collection](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/collection-selection-boxes-postmodern-design) and [April Greiman’s work](https://eyeondesign.aiga.org/april-greiman-is-still-ahead-of-the-curve/) are related visual research, rather than a claim about the cover’s historical influences.

The site applies that energy through hierarchy. The homepage is a cover; writing and work indexes help readers choose; an entry settles into a reading surface. Keep the author and the work as the subjects. Use specific titles, real project images and useful routes into the archive.

- **Expression has a location.** Tilt the homepage headline and featured plate. Keep navigation, controls, tables and paragraphs upright.
- **Colour carries structure.** Cobalt introduces writing and interaction, cream supports reading, green closes the page. Images retain their own colours.
- **Evidence stays near the claim.** The introduction links to delivered work. The work index pairs project imagery with an inspectable record.
- **Space and shared edges do the grouping.** Add a rule where it clarifies a table, a boundary or a control. Leave room between unrelated sections.

## Palette
